let __v_635 = Value::from("Cut a buddleia hard in March, back to about 30cm: it flowers on the shoots that grow from there, so the harder you cut the bigger the spikes. Do it every year, and take out any dead stems at the base at the same time. A neglected one can lose two thirds of its height at once and still come back.");
frame.store("PRUNE_BUDDLEIA", __v_635.clone())?;
let __v_636 = Value::from("Wisteria is pruned twice: in July or August shorten the long whippy shoots to five or six leaves, then in January cut those same shoots back to two or three buds. That is what turns growth into flowers. Leave any shoot you are training out longer, and do not feed it with nitrogen, which gives you leaves instead.");
frame.store("PRUNE_WISTERIA", __v_636.clone())?;
let __v_637 = Value::from("Leave the old flowers on over winter for frost protection, then in March cut each stem back to a fat pair of buds about 30cm up and take out the oldest stems at the base. Do not cut a mophead to the ground: it flowers on last year's wood. A paniculata kind is the exception and flowers best cut hard in spring.");
frame.store("PRUNE_HYDRANGEA", __v_637.clone())?;
let __v_638 = Value::from("Trim an evergreen shrub in mid to late spring, once the frosts are over: bay, holly, laurel and box all take a tidy-up then, and bay takes a light trim in August too. Do not cut back into bare old wood, because most evergreens will not shoot again from it - a hard cut is better spread over two years.");
frame.store("PRUNE_EVERGREEN", __v_638.clone())?;
let __v_639 = Value::from("Summer-fruiting raspberries: cut the canes that fruited this year down to the ground in February and tie in the new ones. Autumn-fruiting ones are easier, because every cane is cut to the ground in February and the fruit comes on the new growth the same year. Take out the weak and crowded canes either way.");
frame.store("PRUNE_RASPBERRY", __v_639.clone())?;
let __v_640 = Value::from("Prune it in late winter or early spring, around March, while it is dormant: it flowers on the shoots that grow this year, so cutting it back then gives the strongest growth and the most flower. Take out dead and crossing stems first, then up to a third of the oldest stems at the base.");
frame.store("PRUNE_LATER", __v_640.clone())?;
let __v_641 = Value::from("Prune it straight after it has flowered, not in winter: it made this year's flower buds last summer, so a spring cut takes the flowers with it. Take the flowered shoots back by about a third as the blooms fade, and leave the rest of the growth alone until next year.");
frame.store("PRUNE_EARLIER", __v_641.clone())?;
