let __v_712 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"monstera\",{\"$tuple\":[\"monstera\",\"monsteras\",\"swiss cheese plant\",\"cheese plant\"]},\"Water a monstera when the top 2 to 3cm of compost is dry, which is about once a week in summer and every two weeks in winter. Give it bright light out of direct sun, and don't worry when water drips from the leaf tips for an hour afterwards: that is the plant pushing out moisture, not a problem.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"pothos\",{\"$tuple\":[\"pothos\",\"devil's ivy\",\"devils ivy\",\"devil ivy\"]},\"Water a pothos about once a week, when the compost is dry and the leaves are just starting to look limp: it forgives a missed week but rots in a pot that never dries. It grows in almost any light, and a cutting in a glass of water roots in a few weeks.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"snake-plant\",{\"$tuple\":[\"snake plant\",\"snake plants\",\"sansevieria\",\"mother in laws tongue\"]},\"Water a snake plant every two to three weeks in summer and once a month or less in winter, and only when the compost is dry all the way down. It stores water in its leaves and rots in wet soil, so the surest way to kill one is to water it on a schedule.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"spider-plant\",{\"$tuple\":[\"spider plant\",\"spider plants\"]},\"Water a spider plant about once a week, when the top of the compost is dry, and let the pot drain. Brown tips are usually tap water or dry air rather than a watering fault: trim them off and stand the pot somewhere less dry.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"peace-lily\",{\"$tuple\":[\"peace lily\",\"peace lilies\",\"spathiphyllum\"]},\"Water a peace lily when the compost is just dry, about once a week, and it will tell you when it wants more by drooping: it picks itself up within a few hours of a drink. It likes a bright spot out of direct sun and brown leaf tips mean the water or the air is hard.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"fiddle-leaf-fig\",{\"$tuple\":[\"fiddle leaf fig\",\"fiddle fig\",\"fiddle leaf\"]},\"Water a fiddle leaf fig when the top 3cm of soil is dry, which is about every ten days, and let it drain right through. It sulks if it is moved or sits in a draught, and brown blotches on the leaves are usually too much water rather than too little.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"zz-plant\",{\"$tuple\":[\"zz plant\",\"zz plants\",\"zamioculcas\"]},\"Water a ZZ plant every two to three weeks in summer and once a month in winter. Its thick underground stems hold water, so it survives neglect far better than kindness: yellow leaves that fall off are the sign of too much.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"aloe\",{\"$tuple\":[\"aloe\",\"aloe vera\"]},\"Water an aloe every two to three weeks in summer and once a month in winter, soaking the pot and then letting it dry out completely. Leaves going soft and pale at the base mean the compost has stayed wet.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"cactus\",{\"$tuple\":[\"cactus\",\"cacti\",\"cactuses\"]},\"Water a cactus every two to three weeks in summer, and barely at all from November to March. Soak it, let it dry right out, and never leave the pot standing in water: more cacti die of watering than of neglect.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"succulent\",{\"$tuple\":[\"succulent\",\"succulents\"]},\"Water a succulent by soaking the pot and then letting it dry out completely: every two to three weeks in summer, monthly in winter. The leaves going soft and see-through at the base is rot from wet compost, and shrivelled leaves are the sign it wants a drink.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"orchid\",{\"$tuple\":[\"orchid\",\"orchids\",\"moth orchid\",\"phalaenopsis\"]},\"Water an orchid by standing the pot in tepid water for ten minutes once a week, then letting it drain: it lives in bark, not compost, so a normal watering can runs straight through. Roots that are silver want water and roots that are green have had enough.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"fern\",{\"$tuple\":[\"fern\",\"ferns\",\"boston fern\",\"maidenhair fern\"]},\"Water a fern when the compost is barely dry, which is twice a week in summer, and stand the pot on pebbles in a saucer of water for the humidity. Crispy brown fronds are dry air, not thirst: a bathroom or a kitchen suits most ferns better than a windowsill.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"rubber-plant\",{\"$tuple\":[\"rubber plant\",\"rubber plants\",\"ficus elastica\"]},\"Water a rubber plant every week to ten days, when the top 3cm of compost is dry, and wipe the leaves so the dust doesn't block the light. Dropping lower leaves one at a time is usually a cold draught or a sudden move.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"jade-plant\",{\"$tuple\":[\"jade plant\",\"jade plants\"]},\"Water a jade plant every two to three weeks in summer and once a month in winter, and let the compost dry between waterings. It wants a sunny windowsill and shrivelled leaves mean thirst while soft, dropping leaves mean too much water.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[\"calathea\",{\"$tuple\":[\"calathea\",\"prayer plant\"]},\"Water a calathea when the top of the compost is just dry, about twice a week in summer, and keep it out of direct sun. Curling leaves and brown edges are dry air: a tray of wet pebbles or a bathroom does more for it than more water does.\"]}]}")?;
